component SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  goal {
    Own compatible streaming of long-running compiler progress and authoritative
    terminal outcomes.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      CompilationEvent is a versioned JSON Lines envelope containing protocolVersion,
      runId, sequence, type, and payload

      protocolVersion 1 is the supported event envelope version

      successful settlement requires a completed terminal event containing the
      authoritative CompilationReport

      a failed terminal event contains the stable rejection code selected by the
      invoking compiler operation
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationEventProtocol {
  constraints {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      JSON Lines mode writes only CompilationEvent envelopes to standard output.
      Human progress and host logs use standard error.

      Sequence values increase within one run and one terminal event ends the stream.
      Failed and cancelled runs emit terminal events only when a compatible event
      stream is writable at the moment the terminal envelope is written.

      A completed method settlement returns the same authoritative CompilationReport
      carried by the completed terminal event. Cancellation returns no
      CompilationReport and rejects with COMPILER_CANCELLED. Other operation failures
      return no report and preserve the invoking component's stable rejection code in
      the failed event and method settlement.

      Own event encoding, framing, ordering, and compatibility, but do not reclassify
      proposal, session, workspace, evaluator, persistence, or transport-loss
      outcomes selected by invoking components. Invalid encoding, framing, ordering,
      or protocol compatibility rejects with COMPILER_FAILED.

      Loss while writing a nonterminal progress event may suppress later progress
      while work continues toward settlement. A terminal event remains mandatory.

      An unavailable stream or failure while writing a completed, cancelled, or
      failed terminal event supersedes the pending outcome with COMPILER_FAILED and
      returns no CompilationReport. Do not attempt another terminal write through the
      failed transport.

      An unsupported event protocol version is a protocol failure, returns no
      CompilationReport, and rejects with COMPILER_FAILED. A failed terminal event is
      emitted only when a compatible writable transport remains available.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      Decision: Use a versioned JSON Lines stream with one authoritative report in
      the completed terminal event.

      Scope: Governs compiler streaming to every host surface.

      Trade-offs: Consumers process an ordered stream instead of one JSON object.

      Discarded alternatives: Mixed stdout and unframed JSON were rejected because
      they cannot safely carry incremental events.

      Consequences: Hosts validate compatibility, ordering, completion, failure, and
      cancellation consistently; component-owned failure identity is preserved when
      its terminal event is written, while terminal transport failure supersedes it.

      Revisit when: A protocol revision requires a compatibility window or negotiated
      transition beyond version 1.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationEventProtocol {
      A successful run emits ordered progress then one completed event containing the
      report.

      Cancelled and failed runs emit their corresponding terminal event when the
      process can still write it.

      Cancellation after work begins rejects with COMPILER_CANCELLED and emits one
      cancelled terminal event when transport remains writable.

      A proposal, session, workspace, evaluator, or persistence failure rejects with
      its invoking component's stable code and emits one failed event carrying that
      code when transport remains writable.

      An encoding, framing, ordering, or protocol compatibility failure rejects with
      COMPILER_FAILED and emits one failed terminal event only when a distinct
      compatible stream remains writable.

      Failure before a writable event stream exists cannot settle through its
      required terminal event and therefore rejects with COMPILER_FAILED.

      Loss while writing progress may omit progress. If the terminal event cannot be
      written, the method rejects with COMPILER_FAILED and returns no report rather
      than exposing completion, cancellation, or the preceding failure identity.

      A consumer receiving an unsupported protocol version rejects the run with
      COMPILER_FAILED before interpreting its payload.
    }
  }
}
